It doesn't, at least until...
- - we create /dev/ttyF*, /dev/ttyG* etc. for multiple wsdisplay =
instances
- - X learns to probe through all of them
neither is very difficult actually, when I have a few days off work =20
I'll probably just write the X part.
Simply treat every wsdisplay instance as its own PCI domain so X won't =20=

try to muck with our BARs, use the PCI config space ioctl()s on the =20
wsdisplay's file handle so we would only see the graphics chip - that =20=

would probably simplify a few thing in the Xserver and to top it off =20
it would be completely MI.

> Also, alpha support graphic adapters which are not VGA (like the TGA =20=

> for example)

Same goes for macppc and sparc64 - we don't even use vga at pci. Ever.
All this requires is some code in <whatever>_mmap() to allow mmap()ing =20=

PCI resources as their bus addresses and optionally map PCI IO space. =20=

All PCI framebuffer drivers we use on macppc or sparc64 do just that. =20=

Adding it to TGA is trivial.

>> If you need PIO access define PCI_MAGIC_IO_RANGE to something never =20=

>> used by PCI memory resources, the necessary voodoo is already in =20
>> place in vga_pci.c.
